From d982be88c1c145bf1b38758ade8f30d68992a10d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 17:51:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Say that the update check sends the version, because it does

Two comments claimed the request carries no version of its own. It does, and it
always has: the User-Agent is set to Bare or Bare/<version> a few lines below
the sentence saying otherwise.

The point the comments were reaching for is still true and is now stated as it
stands. The request carries the product and its version and nothing else that
identifies anything: no cookies, no cache, no device model, no Android build,
no installation identifier. The platform default User-Agent would have carried
the model and the build, which is why it is replaced rather than left alone.

Comments and tests only. The request itself is unchanged. The tests cover the
version ordering this release turns on, since a build that reads its own release
tag as newer than itself would offer an update forever.
---
 .../chrome/browser/bare/BareUpdateCheck.java  | 13 ++++++-----
 .../browser/bare/BareUpdateCheckUnitTest.java | 22 +++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 29 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/chrome/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareUpdateCheck.java b/chrome/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareUpdateCheck.java
index 5d0eaee26b3..90bcd96f74c 100644
--- a/chrome/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareUpdateCheck.java
+++ b/chrome/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareUpdateCheck.java
@@ -32,9 +32,9 @@ import java.util.regex.Pattern;
  *
  * <p>Bare ships outside any store. Nothing else tells a user that a fix exists, so this asks.
  * It is also the only thing in the browser that contacts a server the user did not navigate to,
- * which is why it stays small: one plain request to a public release feed, no identifier, no
- * version number in the request, nothing scheduled in the background. A check happens while the
- * browser is already open and already using the network, or when the user asks for one.
+ * so it stays small: one plain request to a public release feed, carrying the product and its
+ * version and no other identifier, with nothing scheduled in the background. A check happens
+ * while the browser is already open and already using the network, or when the user asks.
  *
  * <p>Manual mode means manual. Nothing leaves the device until the user taps, and the weekly
  * reminder is a local timer, not a request.
@@ -239,9 +239,10 @@ public class BareUpdateCheck {
     /**
      * Reads the first release tag out of the feed.
      *
-     * <p>Deliberately plain: no cookies, no cache, no identifying header, and no version of its own
-     * in the request. The server learns an address asked for a public file, which is the least this
-     * can cost while still working.
+     * <p>Plain: no cookies, no cache, and no identifying header. The one thing the request does
+     * say about this install is the Bare version, in the User-Agent, so a release can be matched
+     * to the builds asking for it. The server learns an address asked for a public file and the
+     * version it is running, and nothing about the device it runs on.
      */
     private static @Nullable String fetchLatestTag() {
         HttpURLConnection connection = null;
diff --git a/chrome/android/junit/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareUpdateCheckUnitTest.java b/chrome/android/junit/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareUpdateCheckUnitTest.java
